#chapter("Overview", image: image("./assets/hd3-2481-500.svg"), l: "chap1")
#index("Sectioning")

The Kurzweil API lives in the `midi-workshop-api` module, under the package `sysex.manufacturer.kurzweil`. It handles
two different kinds of data:

- *SysEx messages*, exchanged live with the unit over MIDI. They are parsed by `KurzweilSysExParser`.
- *Disk/PRAM files*, read from a file or an image. They are parsed by `KurzweilFileReader` and can be converted back to
  SysEx by `KurzweilFileConverter`.

Both paths share the same data model (see the next chapter) and the same binary decoders (see the chapter on
deserializers).

The main pipelines are:

#figure(
  table(
    align: left,
    columns: (auto, 1fr),
    fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { luma(230) },
    stroke: 0.5pt,
    table.header([*Pipeline*], [*Classes involved*]),
    [File to objects], [`KurzweilFileReader` to `KFHeader`, `KFObjectDeserializer` to `KFProgram` / `KFSoundBlock` / `KFKeyMap`...],
    [SysEx to objects], [`KurzweilSysExParser` to `ObjectWrite` / `ObjectLoad` / `ObjectInfo`, then the same deserializers],
    [Objects to SysEx], [`KurzweilFileConverter.toSysEx`, which writes one `.syx` file per program],
  ),
  caption: [Main API pipelines],
)

The module targets the K2600 family: `Product.K2600` has code `0x78`, which is also the value written in the header of
the SysEx messages generated by the converter. A second product code, `K2700` (`0x02`), is declared but not used by the
current code.

== Commands

SysEx messages carry a command byte. The `Command` enum lists all of them, but only a few are handled by the parser:

#figure(
  table(
    align: left,
    columns: (auto, auto, 1fr),
    fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { luma(230) },
    stroke: 0.5pt,
    table.header([*Command*], [*Code*], [*Handling in `KurzweilSysExParser`*]),
    [`DUMP`], [`0x00`], [Parsed, but the result is not returned yet (`null`)],
    [`LOAD`], [`0x01`], [Parsed into `ObjectLoad` (offset, size, format, unpacked payload)],
    [`DACK`], [`0x02`], [Parsed into `DataACK`],
    [`DNACK`], [`0x03`], [Parsed into `DataNACK`],
    [`INFO`], [`0x05`], [Parsed into `ObjectInfo` (type, id, size, RAM flag, name)],
    [`WRITE`], [`0x09`], [Parsed into `ObjectWrite`, then deserialized for a few object types],
    [`SCREEN_REPLY`], [`0x19`], [Text dump (320 characters) or bitmap dump (240 x 64 pixels)],
    [Others], [], [Declared in the enum, returned as `null`],
  ),
  caption: [Kurzweil SysEx commands],
)

== Known limitations

- Only a subset of object types is deserialized (see the chapter on deserializers). Other types raise an exception in the
  file reader, or are silently ignored in the SysEx parser.
- The stream formats `STREAM` (7 bits per byte) and `NIBBLE` (two bytes per data byte) are both supported when reading. The
  converter only writes `NIBBLE`.

#chapter("Data model", image: image("./assets/hd3-2481-500.svg"), l: "chap1")
#index("Sectioning")

== Object types (`KObject`)

Kurzweil identifies every object by a type and an object ID. The `KObject` enum maps each type to its numeric ID as
declared in the official `objtypes.h`. The enum also exposes a packed form (`getType()`), used on the MIDI side, where
the bit 8 is always 0.

#figure(
