#chapter("Deserializers", image: image("./assets/hd3-2481-500.svg"), l: "chap1")
#index("Sectioning")

All the binary decoding is done by subclasses of `KFDeserializer`. The base class provides the helpers shared by all of
them: reading and writing the object name, reading magic strings and unsigned integers, and dumping an object to JSON in
the `target` folder for debugging.

== Object names

Names use an "offset to data" convention. The first 16-bit value is the length of the name block, including the 2 bytes
of the offset itself. The name is ASCII, null terminated, and padded so that the block length is even and at most 18
bytes. `readName` and `writeName` follow this rule.

== Reading a file (`KurzweilFileReader`)

`read()` runs in three steps:

+ It reads the 32-byte header (`KFHeaderDeserializer`), which contains the magic string and the sample data offset.
+ It reads blocks until the size is non-negative. A block size is stored as a negative number that includes itself:
  the real size is `abs(size) - 4`.
+ For each block, `KFObjectDeserializer.deserializeObjects` decodes the objects, then the PCM data of every sound block
  is extracted at `offsetSampleData + sampleStart * byteDepth`.

Each extracted PCM is written to `target/sample_<id>_<rate>KHz_16Bits_BE_<length>_<name>.pcm`. The whole file is
dumped to `target/kurzweil_dump_<file>.json`.

== Object header

Inside a block, each object starts with:

- a 16-bit identifier. If the highest bit is set, the object type takes 5 bits and the object ID takes 10 bits.
  Otherwise the type takes 7 bits and the object ID takes 8 bits,
- a 16-bit size, which includes the 4 bytes of the header.

`KFObjectDeserializer` decodes this header and calls the matching deserializer. Types other than `PROGRAM`,
`SOUND_BLOCK`, `KEYMAP`, `STUDIO` and `TABLE` (master parameters) raise an `IllegalArgumentException`.

== Programs and segments

`KFProgramDeserializer` reads the program name, then `KFProgramSegmentDeserializer` walks the segments. Each segment is
read by the deserializer declared in `ProgramSegmentType` (for example `KFCalvinDeserializer` for `CALSEGTAG` or
`KFHobbesDeserializer` for `HOBSEGTAG`). Serialization follows the same classes in reverse order, using a
`BitStreamWriter`.

== Sound blocks

`KFSoundBlockDeserializer` reads the sound block fields (`base`, `nsfh`, `off`, `sflags`, `copyID`, `rfu`), then
`nsfh + 1` headers, then the envelopes. The number of envelopes is computed from the remaining bytes, with 12 bytes per
envelope.

== SysEx messages (`KurzweilSysExParser`)

`parse(byte[])` splits the input into individual SysEx messages, then for each one:

+ checks the manufacturer code (`0x07` for Kurzweil),
+ reads the device ID, the product code and the command,
+ dispatches to the matching `parseXXX` method.

For `WRITE` and `LOAD`, the payload is decoded according to its format:

#figure(
  table(
    align: left,
    columns: (auto, 1fr),
    fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { luma(230) },
    stroke: 0.5pt,
    table.header([*Format*], [*Decoding*]),
    [`STREAM`], [7 bits per byte: the first bit of each 8-bit group is skipped, the next 7 bits are kept],
    [`NIBBLE`], [Two bytes per byte: the first nibble is skipped, the second nibble is kept],
  ),
  caption: [Stream formats],
)

The checksum is a simple sum of the payload bytes (`SimpleSumChecksum`). The decoded bytes are also written to
`target/` (`Packed ...syx` and `Unpacked ....dat`) for analysis.

#chapter("Converter", image: image("./assets/hd3-2481-500.svg"), l: "chap1")
#index("Sectioning")

`KurzweilFileConverter.toSysEx` turns the objects of a loaded file into MIDI SysEx files that can be sent to a K2600.
It produces one `.syx` file per program. The output folder also receives the sample data (`.pcm`) and a debug dump of
each program (`.unpacked`).

== Selecting the programs

Only the programs that use at least one keymap with an ID of 200 or more are converted. The keymaps and sound blocks
referenced by these programs are written before the program itself.

== Building the program

The converter does not send the program as it is stored in the file. It first loads the template
`/kurzweil/k2600r-default-program.json` and:

+ creates one layer for each layer of the source program,
+ merges the source program into the template with `TreeMerger`. Non-null source fields overwrite the template, and
  lists are merged index by index,
+ forces the program format to 4.

The result is a complete program, with every field present, which the unit accepts over MIDI.

== Keymaps and sound blocks

Before writing, the converter forces the keymap method to `0x17` and the entry size to 6 bytes. This means that every
entry carries the tuning (2 bytes), the sample ID (2 bytes), the volume attenuation (1 byte) and the sample root
(1 byte). Keymaps are therefore rewritten, even if the original file used fewer fields.

== SysEx message format

Each object is written as a `WRITE` command:

#figure(
  table(
    align: left,
    columns: (auto, auto, 1fr),
    fill: (x, y) => if y == 0 { luma(230) },
    stroke: 0.5pt,
    table.header([*Field*], [*Size*], [*Content*]),
    [Start, manufacturer, device, product], [4], [`F0 07 00 78`],
    [Command], [1], [`09` (`WRITE`)],
    [Object type], [2], [Packed type, big endian],
    [Object ID], [2], [14 bits, two 7-bit bytes],
    [Unpacked size], [3], [24 bits, three 7-bit bytes],
    [Override mode], [1], [`00`],
    [Name], [variable], [ASCII, null terminated],
    [Format], [1], [`00` (`NIBBLE`)],
    [Payload], [variable], [Each byte split into two nibbles],
    [Checksum], [1], [Simple sum of the payload],
    [End], [1], [`F7`],
  ),
  caption: [Layout of a `WRITE` message],
)

After writing, `checkGeneratedSysEx` parses the generated file again with `KurzweilSysExParser`, which validates the
message structure.

== Output files

For a program with ID `n` and name `name`, the converter writes:

- `n - name.syx`: the SysEx file, with the sample blocks, keymaps and program in this order,
- `n - name.unpacked`: the raw program segments, for debugging,
- `id - sampleName.pcm`: the PCM data of each sample.
